<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=fired-brick">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fired brick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5000–4500 BCE</span> · Also known as Baked brick, Burned brick, Ceramic brick</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fired brick is brick of clay and shale baked in a kiln, harder and longer-lasting than the older sun-dried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> had discovered it between 5000 and 4500 BCE, and the earliest fired bricks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> appeared around 4400 BCE at Chengtoushan, a walled settlement of the Daxi culture, where they paved house floors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> cities such as Kalibangan used it by 3000 BCE, and the Roman legions stamped the bricks of their mobile kilns across the empire. The first brick-making machine was patented in 1852, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> multiplied production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fired-brick.jpg" alt="Fired brick" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lungoleno · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mattone_-_cotto_ferrarese_-_Ferrara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Bhagwanpura, <b>fired bricks</b> may have been used in Vedic altars.</blockquote><a href="/culture/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware culture · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>5000–4500 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Mesopotamia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Chengtoushan, China (c. 4400 BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Fired bricks are also said to have been used only since c. 4000 BCE. In China proper brick construction of walls and vaults emerged only in the third century BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/pottery">Pottery<span>c. 18,000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">Mudbrick<span>c. 9000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=fired-brick">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
